@article{FinkinDisloyalty2007,
  title = {Disloyalty! Does Jefferson Standard Stalk Still?},
  author = {Finkin, Matthew W.},
  year = {2007},
  journal = {Berkeley J. Emp. \& Lab. L.},
  volume = {28},
  number = {2},
  pages = {541},
  keywords = {employee-loyalty,ObsCite,protected-concerted-activity},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (2/10/2023, 5:39:10 PM)}
\par
"The Board majority in Endicott Interconnect opened with a brief summary the Board had previously essayed in Mountain Shadows Golf Resort:" a communication directed to a third party is protected if it indicates its connection to a labor dispute and "is not so disloyal, reckless or maliciously untrue" as to lose protection." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=6}{Finkin 2007:546})
\par
"personal fealty to management has sometimes been an element of corporate policy.19" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=8}{Finkin 2007:548})
\par
"The law of master and servant implied on the servant's part a basic obligation of faithfulness to the master's interests. As Lord Kenyon put in 1799, "A servant while engaged in the service of his master, has no right to do any act which may injure his trade, or undermine his business ... 20 This is the obligation captured by the catchphrase "disloyalty."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=8}{Finkin 2007:548})
\par
"GERALD ZAHAVI, WORKERS, MANAGERS, AND WELFARE CAPITALISM: THE SHOEWORKERS AND TANNERS OF ENDIcOTr JOHNSON, 1890-1950, 41 (1988). Zahavi explains: "Loyalty meant a personal allegiance to management as well as a commitment to the good of the corporation." Id. (emphasis added). Personal fealty to the employer was an express component of labor law in Nazi Germany. TIM MASON, NAZISM, FASCISM AND THE WORKING CLASS 77-103 (Jane Caplan ed., 1995)" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=8}{Finkin 2007:548})
\par
"a servant's conduct that is likely to produce detriment to the master breaches the employment contract and gives cause to dismiss. Breach had been found where the servant's conduct had been calculated to offend, annoy, or alarm persons having business dealings with his master; where he had made a wilful and deliberate attempt to injure his master's business; where his acts or words had been such as to injure the master's standing as a business man; where he had acquired an interest in a business which competed with that of his master; where he had taken part in a strike affecting the employer's interests; where his behavior was calculated to excite discontent among his subordinates.2" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=9}{Finkin 2007:549})
\par
"As Justice Frankfurter noted in Jefferson Standard, much that the Labor Act protects would be considered in breach of the implied obligation that a servant do nothing likely to produce detriment to the master's business. As Labatt's treatise pointed out, taking part in a strike would be just such an act and he cited authority to just that effect;3 but, as Frankfurter noted, an economic strike is quintessentially protected labor activity." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=10}{Finkin 2007:550})
\par
"Today, private employment is hedged 'round with whistleblower laws and common law tort doctrine protecting employees from discharge for speaking out and so encouraging them to do so, i.e., to breach their duty of loyalty. 5" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=13}{Finkin 2007:553})
\par
"In the Board's view the speech was disloyal because, just as in Jefferson Standard, the drivers spoke to a matter that is not their proper concern as employees." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=26}{Finkin 2007:566})
\par
"Toering Electric Co.," (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=27}{Finkin 2007:567})
\par
"held that no unfair labor practice was worked by an employer's refusal to hire "salted" applicants-persons sent by unions to apply for jobs who had no interest in actually taking them. The soundness of that holding is beyond the scope of this discussion. But the Board majority bolstered its reasoning by citation to and reliance on Jefferson Standard: "The Board does not serve its intended statutory role . . . if it must litigate hiring" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=27}{Finkin 2007:567})
\par
"discrimination charges filed on behalf of disingenuous applicants who intend no service and loyalty to a common enterprise. ... Intending no loyalty to a prospective employer now exempts not only the applicant from the Act's reach, it exempts the employer from scrutiny of its hiring policies." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/3C57HQK8?page=28}{Finkin 2007:568})},
  file = {/home/efink/Notes/Zotero/PDF/DataTrespass/Loyalty/@FinkinDisloyalty2007.pdf}
}

