\par
"It is clear what fact creates liability under the Act, the breach *583 of the duty of loyalty. Any subsequent difficulties or close calls in deciding whether a breach has in fact occurred does not amount to a vagueness issue." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/9ANDGDAV?page=18}{ 2020:18})
\par
"the lack of a precise definition of a prohibited act does not render a law void for vagueness." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/9ANDGDAV?page=18}{ 2020:18})
\par
\emph{But the ambiguity about the employee duty of loyalty under NC does heighten concerns that the statute could apply to PCA. (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/9ANDGDAV?page=18}{note on p.18})}
\par
"the court finds that subsections (b)(1) and (b)(5) of the Property Protection Act are unconstitutional as applied to Plaintiffs and subsections (b)(2) and (b)(3) are unconstitutional both facially and as applied." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/9ANDGDAV?page=21}{ 2020:21})
