@techreport{GC21-03_EffectuationOfTheNLRA2021,
  type = {General Counsel Memo},
  title = {Effectuation of the National Labor Relations Act Through Vigorous Enforcement of the Mutual Aid or Protection and Inherently Concerted Doctrines},
  year = {2021},
  month = mar,
  number = {GC 21-03},
  institution = {National Labor Relations Board},
  keywords = {ObsCite,protected-concerted-activity},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (6/19/2023, 3:25:36 PM)}
\par
"MEMORANDUM GC 21-03" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=1}{ 2021:1})
\par
"March 31, 2021" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=1}{ 2021:1})
\par
"Effectuation of the National Labor Relations Act Through Vigorous Enforcement of the Mutual Aid or Protection and Inherently Concerted Doctrines" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=1}{ 2021:1})
\par
"While protected, concerted activity can be a precursor to a union campaign, it also can occur outside of the context of union activity, such as in instances where employees raise safety concerns to their employer, or seek protection from government agencies. constraints and limitations placed on employees engaging in concerted activities, and adverse actions taken against them in response to their protected activity, serve to effectively undermine the declared policy of the United States." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=1}{ 2021:1})
\par
""mutual aid or protection. "" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=2}{ 2021:2})
\par
"The latter element "focuses on the goal of concerted activity," specifically, "whether there is a link between the activity and matters concerning the workplace or employees' interests as employees. 1 The Board analyzes whether an activity is for "mutual aid or protection" using an objective standard; thus, employees' subjective motives are irrelevant.2 The "mutual aid or protection" clause covers employee efforts to "improve their lot as employees through channels outside the immediate employee-employer relationship," as well as activities "in support of employees of employers other than their own. 3" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=2}{ 2021:2})
\par
"Section 7 protection applies not only to union activity and labor organizing; it may also cover the fundamental precursor actions that form the cornerstone of any other actions the employees may take, like discussing or protesting wages, hours, and working conditions. employee advocacy can have the goal of "mutual aid or protection" even when the employees have not explicitly connected their activity to workplace concerns." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=2}{ 2021:2})
\par
"Finding Certain Conduct to be Inherently Concerted" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=4}{ 2021:4})
\par
"Protected, concerted activity - either standing alone or as a precursor to organizational activity - begins with a conversation among employees." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=4}{ 2021:4})
\par
"Conduct generally becomes concerted when it is "engaged in with or on the authority of other employees," or when an employee seeks either "to initiate or to induce or to prepare for group action.  the touchstone of concert revolves around employees' intention to band together to improve their wages or working conditions." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=4}{ 2021:4})
\par
"While contemplation of group action may be indicative of concerted activity, it is not a required element. Employee discussions of certain "vital elements of employment"18 often raise concerns that are pivotal to their collective interests, which, in some circumstances, may spur organizational considerations. Concern about these crucial common issues may render group discussions inherently concerted, "even if group action is nascent or not yet contemplated." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=5}{ 2021:5})
\par
"Employees who share information with each other about wages or wage differentials may be acting in an inherently concerted manner, insofar as,"[i]t is obvious that higher wages are a frequent objective of organizational activity.  Discussion among employees of changes in their work schedules implicates vital elements of employment such as hours and working conditions, and thus "are as likely to spawn collective action as the discussion of wages.  job security is a "vital term and condition of employment," which involves "the very existence of an employment relationship," e.g., whether an employee may be laid off or discharged. The Division of Advice has further concluded tha" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=5}{ 2021:5})
\par
"discussions concerning workplace health and safety25 and racial discrimination26 may be inherently concerted." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=6}{ 2021:6})
\par
"North West Rural Electric Cooperative, Case 18-CA-150605, Advice Memorandum dated September 21, 2015, at 9-12. In North West Rural Electric Cooperative, 366 NLRB No. 132, slip op. 1, n.1 (2018), the Board declined to pass on the judge's conclusion that an employee's discussion about safety issues in the electrical lineman industry over Facebook was inherently concerted, in light of an alternate theory of concert." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=6}{ 2021:6})
\par
"Milford Center, Case 01-CA-156820, Advice Memorandum dated January 20, 2016, at 9-12. The Division of Advice noted that, "[a]n employer's racial bias or discrimination [...] implicates all terms and conditions of employment---including, but not limited to, those the Board has already identified as being inherently concerted, such as wages, work schedules, and job security. " Id. at 11." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/4GR9N8M6?page=6}{ 2021:6})},
  file = {/home/efink/Notes/Zotero/PDF/DataTrespass/Protected Concerted Activity/@GC21-03_EffectuationOfTheNLRA2021.pdf}
}

@misc{MeyersI1984,
  title = {Meyers Industries, Inc. ("Meyers I")},
  shorttitle = {Meyers I},
  year = {1984},
  volume = {268},
  pages = {493},
  keywords = {ObsCite,protected-concerted-activity},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (6/19/2023, 3:25:50 PM)}
\par
"Meyers Industries, Inc" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=1}{ 1984:1})
\par
"I. THE CONCEPT OF PROTECTED CONCERTED ACTIVITY" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=1}{ 1984:1})
\par
"Although the legislative history of Section 7 does not specifically define "concerted activity," it does reveal that Congress considered the concept in terms of individuals united in pursuit of a common goal." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=1}{ 1984:1})
\par
"the statute envisions "concerted" action in terms of collective activity: the formation of or assistance to a group, or action as a representative on behalf of a group. Section 7 limits the employee rights it grants to the examples of concerted activities specifically enumerated therein---"self-organization"; forming, joining, or assisting labor organizations; and bargaining collectively through representatives---and to engaging in "other concerted activities for the purpose of collective bargaining or other mutual aid or protection." (Emphasis added.) Thus, the statute requires that the activities in question be "concerted" before they can be "protected." Indeed, Section 7 does not use the term "protected concerted activities," but only "concerted activities."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=2}{ 1984:2})
\par
"IV. DEFINITION OF CONCERTED ACTIVITY" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=5}{ 1984:5})
\par
"In general, to find an employee's activity to be "concerted," we shall require that it be engaged in with or on the authority of other employees, and not solely by and on behalf of the employee himself.22 Once the activity is found to be concerted, an 8(a)(1) violation will be found if, in addition, the employer knew of the concerted nature of the employee's activity, the concerted activity was protected by the Act, and the adverse employment action at issue (e.g., discharge) was motivated by the employee's protected concerted activity" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/DYJPH734?page=5}{ 1984:5})},
  file = {/home/efink/Notes/Zotero/PDF/DataTrespass/Protected Concerted Activity/@MeyersI1984.pdf}
}

