  number = {5},
  pages = {1705},
  keywords = {employee-loyalty,ObsCite,protected-concerted-activity},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (11/6/2023, 1:02:36 PM)}
\par
"DISLOYAL WORKERS AND THE "UN-AMERICAN" LABOR LAW" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=1}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1705})
\par
"Loyalty is a check on exit.7 Hirschman defines loyalty as "the reluctance to exit in spite of disagreement with the organization of which one is a member."8 Loyalty describes the psychological bond that influences us to transcend passing discontent and self-interest, persuades us to further the welfare of the relationship or institution over our individual concerns, and motivates us to stay and voice concerns rather than to alleviate conflict through exit.9 The exit option is nonetheless a critical complement to loyalty, providing the leverage for voice as an instrument of change within the" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=3}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1707})
\par
\emph{Data trespass laws use loyalty as a restraint on voice. (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=3}{note on p.1707})}
\par
"relationship.1"" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=4}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1708})
\par
"group action by workers, and particularly the organization and activities of labor unions, is viewed with suspicion and seen as fundamentally disloyal-not only toward employers, but vis-A-vis the state." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=4}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1708})
\par
"II. COLLECTIVE ACTION As DISLOYALTY AND TREASON" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=7}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1711})
\par
"American courts viewed worker organization and collective pressure activities as disloyal acts by servants toward their masters." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=8}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1712})
\par
"worker organizing and pressure strategies evidenced not only disloyalty to the employer, but contempt for the state and the law itself." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=8}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1712})
\par
"IV. CONCEPTIONS OF LOYALTY IN THE POST-NLRA WORKPLACE" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=22}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1726})
\par
"worker protests that disparage in any way the employer's products are deemed unprotected under labor law, despite the explicit (and constitutionally protected) right to picket and boycott. NLRB v. Local Union No. 1229,1" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=22}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1726})
\par
"The essence of loyalty is not exit, but voice. Rather than quitting when she is dissatisfied, the loyal employee voices her discontent." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=45}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1749})
\par
"HIRSCHMAN, supra note 5, at 76-78 (observing that in the face of discontent, the loyal member of any organization chooses voice over exit)" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/7B8GSCX4?page=45}{Matheny and Crain 2004:1749})},
