@article{mcginleyWaterCoolerSpeech2012b,
  title = {Beyond the Water Cooler: Speech and the Workplace in an Era of Social Media},
  author = {McGinley, Ann C and {McGinley-Stempel}, Ryan P},
  year = {2012},
  journal = {Hofstra Labor \& Employment Law Journal},
  volume = {30},
  pages = {75},
  keywords = {ObsCite,protected-concerted-activity},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (6/19/2023, 3:25:27 PM)}
\par
"What many employers and employees may not know is that Section 7 applies to the concerted action of all employees, whether in unionized workplaces, or whether or not they are engaged in a union campaign.7" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=15}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:89})
\par
"Defining "Concerted" and "Protected" in the Social Media Context" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=16}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:90})
\par
"an individual can be engaged in concerted activity if he or she engages in the activity "'with or on the authority of other employees, and not solely by and on behalf of the employee himself.'"8" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=16}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:90})
\par
"individual activities that are the "logical outgrowth of concerns expressed by the employees collectively" are concerted under the Act. concerted activity when record evidence demonstrates group activities whether or not they are "specifically authorized." "[c]oncerted activity also includes 'circumstances where individual employees seek to initiate or to induce or to prepare for group action' and where individual employees bring 'truly group complaints' to management's attention."8" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=16}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:90})
\par
"b. "Concerted Activity"" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=22}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:96})
\par
"As a result of a citizen complaint, Souza's supervisor asked her to write up a report.'45 Knowing that the report might be the first step toward disciplinary action, Souza asked to have a union representative help her fill out the report.146 Her supervisor refused and Souza did not completely fill out the report but wrote on the report only that she had requested and was denied a union representative.147 Later that day, Souza posted comments on her Facebook page concerning her confrontation with her supervisor.148 The comments, to which other employees responded, included reference to her supervisor as a "dick," "scumbag," and a "17" (American Medical Response ("AMR") code for psychiatric patient).149 Souza was suspended from work the following day and terminated at the beginning of the next month.o50 The termination letter stated that AMR had received complaints from the emergency staff of a local hospital, and also stated that Souza had refused to fill out the requested report, and, finally, that Souza had posted derogatory remarks about her supervisor on Facebook.1" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=22}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:96})
\par
"The GC concluded that AMR had violated Souza's Weingarten rights when it refused to allow her a union representative to fill out the" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=22}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:96})
\par
"incident report.1" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=23}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:97})
\par
"Souza's comments on Facebook, moreover, were protected, concerted activity. According to the GC, "[i]t is well established that the protest of supervisory actions is protected conduct under Section 7," 156 and it was clear that the employer knew about the protected activity because it referred to it in the termination letter." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=23}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:97})
\par
"Karl Knauz Motors,18 the charging party was a salesman at a BMW dealership who was terminated for comments and photos posted on his Facebook page. 59 The comments dealt with two events. First, the employer had held a sales event in which BMW served what the charging party and other sales personnel believed to be inadequate food and beverages. 60 The second event involved an accident at a Land Rover store owned by the employer and adjacent to the BMW dealership.161 A child had accidentally driven a new Land Rover into an artificial pond in front of the Land Rover dealership.162 The GC argued that the comments and pictures the employee posted on Facebook, in which he criticized the employer for serving inadequate food and drink, were protected because the employee and other employees had complained to the employer that the sales promotion was inadequate and they were concerned that a poorly run event would affect their sales" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=23}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:97})
\par
"commissions." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"At the event, the employee took photographs to document the event and capture his coworker's frustration. He told his coworkers that he would put the photographs on Facebook, and in doing so, expressed the sentiment of the group." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"The ALJ agreed that the Facebook postings regarding the sales event were protected, concerted activities, but he concluded that the employer fired the employee for the unprotected activity of posting the Land Rover photographs rather than the comments and photos on the BMW sales event.'" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"The Board upheld the AL's decision and concluded that the speech on Facebook concerning the sales event was protected, but also agreed that the employee was not fired for that speech.1" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"Karl Knauz Motors, Inc., Case 13-CA-46452, at 9 (Sept. 28, 2011) (ALJ decision), available at http://www.nlrb.gov/case/13ca46452." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"Karl Knauz Motors, Inc., 358 N.L.R.B. No. 164, 2012 WL 4482841, at *1-2 (Sept. 28, 2012)." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=24}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:98})
\par
"the NLRB has found that calling a supervisor a "liar and a bitch," a "fucking son of a bitch" and an "egotistical flick" did not destroy the protection of the communication." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=31}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:105})
\par
"the employer's threats to file a lawsuit against the employees were unlawful activities that interfere with employees' Section 7 rights. Because the employer did not file a lawsuit but merely threatened to do so, the threat had no First Amendment protection, and even if there were a reasonable basis for the potential legal action, the threats were unlawful.2" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/NIQRQN29?page=32}{McGinley and McGinley-Stempel 2012:106})},
  file = {/home/efink/Notes/Zotero/PDF/DataTrespass/Protected Concerted Activity/@mcginleyWaterCoolerSpeech2012b.pdf}
}

