@misc{GilbertFlandreauSantee2006,
  title = {Gilbert v. Flandreau Santee Sioux Tribe},
  shorttitle = {Gilbert},
  year = {2006},
  month = nov,
  volume = {725},
  pages = {249},
  urldate = {2024-04-12},
  keywords = {first-amendment,law,unemployment},
  note = {\textbf{Extracted Annotations (5/22/2024, 11:15:43 AM)}
\par
"725 N.W.2d 249 (2006)" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"Supreme Court of South Dakota" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"Gilbert was denied unemployment insurance (UI) benefits after a referee determined she had been terminated for work-related misconduct under SDCL *252 61-6-14.1." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"Helen Gilbert (Gilbert) was employed by the Flandreau Santee Sioux Tribe (Tribe) as an education coordinator" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"Gilbert's discharge was based on a letter she wrote to the Tribal Executive Committee in reference to the committee's political appointment of Donalda "Neldie" Montoya (Montoya) to the Office of Tribal Secretary, an office also sought by Gilbert. The letter sharply criticized Montoya's appointment. Gilbert's complaint about Montoya stemmed from an earlier incident concerning defamatory statements Montoya allegedly had made about Gilbert." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"After learning of Montoya's appointment and before writing the letter, Gilbert telephoned Montoya. In the phone call, Gilbert told Montoya that she was going to "sue her for slander for the statement she made in May, and that she should write this conversation date and time down and let ... her supervisor know."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"After the phone call, Gilbert wrote a letter during work hours on tribal stationery to the Tribal Executive Committee. The heading of the letter indicated that it was to the Flandreau Santee Sioux Tribe from Helen Gilbert, education coordinator, in reference to "8/10/04 Incident-Neldie Montoya appointment as FSST Secretary."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"Neldie, during the election petition process had stated to Elizabeth Shortman that the Council" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=1}{ 2006:1})
\par
"was going to block my nomination based on allegations that I was charged as a prostitute. The knowingly false charge such as this sent me over the edge." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"Additionally, the letter criticized the tribal chairman of neglect of duty" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"She then went on to name several other employees who she thought were problem employees and pointedly accused the employees of such things as "violent, hysterical public performances," "violence in the workplace," "violation of the political activity policy," "embezzlement," drug and alcohol violations, and incompetence." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"As a result of sending the letter to the executive committee, the Tribe decided to suspend Gilbert for violating the political activity policy" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"The Department initially determined that the conditions of Gilbert's termination did not disqualify her from receiving UI benefits. The Tribe appealed the Department's determination. Gilbert filed for UI benefits" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"Gilbert claimed that she wrote the letter to the tribal council in her capacity as education coordinator and followed the chain of command before submitting it. After a hearing on the matter, the referee denied Gilbert UI benefits based on a finding that she had been discharged for work-connected misconduct as defined by SDCL 61-6-14.1." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"Gilbert's only contention was that she could not be denied UI benefits based upon work-connected misconduct, i.e. violation of the political activity policy, because of her constitutional right to freedom of speech." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"there was no question that Gilbert's letter violated the political activity policy and that she violated the policy during working hours utilizing tribal stationery" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"Gilbert appeals the decision claiming that her letter to the tribal council is constitutionally protected free speech for which she cannot be disqualified from receiving UI benefits." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=2}{ 2006:2})
\par
"the only issue before us is whether her federal or state constitutional right to freedom of speech protects her from being denied UI benefits." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"The United States Supreme Court has, however, recognized that the First Amendment protects religious freedom in the context of UI benefits. In Sherbert v. Verner, the Court found that a claimant's refusal to work on her faith's Sabbath day was protected and could not serve as a basis for denying UI benefits. Additionally, the Court has long recognized a public employee's protection of freedom of speech in cases of discharge from employment. As recently stated in Garcetti v. Ceballos, "It is well settled that 'a State cannot condition public employment on a basis that infringes the employee's constitutionally protected interest in freedom of expression.'"" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
""[T]he First Amendment protects a public employee's right, in certain circumstances, to speak as a citizen addressing matters of public concern." Id. Thus in determining whether Gilbert can be denied UI benefits, we follow the method of inquiry set forth by the United States Supreme Court." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"two inquiries to guide interpretation of the constitutional protections accorded to public employee speech The first requires determining whether the employee spoke as a citizen on a matter of public concern." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"If the answer is yes, then the possibility of a First Amendment claim arises. The question becomes whether the relevant government entity had an adequate justification for treating the employee differently from any other member of the general public" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"A government entity has broader discretion to restrict speech when it acts in its role as employer, but the restrictions it imposes must be directed at speech that has some potential to affect the entity's operations." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"The United States Supreme Court defined public concern "as relating to any matter of political, social, or other concern to the community."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=3}{ 2006:3})
\par
"Gilbert claims in her response letter to the Department that she wrote the letter to the tribal council in her "capacity as education coordinator." In Garcetti v. Ceballos, the United States Supreme Court held that "when public employees make statements pursuant to their official duties, the employees are not speaking as citizens for First Amendment purposes."" (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=4}{ 2006:4})
\par
"we conclude that when Gilbert wrote the letter, she was not speaking as a citizen on matters of public concern." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=4}{ 2006:4})
\par
"Since Gilbert's statements were not of public concern, the First Amendment of the United States Constitution is not applicable and our inquiry ends." (\href{zotero://open-pdf/library/items/YIN8JBPE?page=5}{ 2006:5})},
  file = {/home/efink/Notes/Zotero/PDF/Labor&Employment/UnemploymentCompensation&Political Activity/@GilbertFlandreauSantee2006.pdf}
}

